From e9cb4ea2daaf390de6b8c2af5df49d214b0b9560 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Kryxzort <101042137+Kryxzort@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 6 Nov 2026 14:58:58 +0530
Subject: [PATCH 105/117] render: a simulation effect steps at the host's frame
 rate, not per frame drawn
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

cursorripple (apply_force, simulate_force, combine) carries its water in two of its own FBOs from
frame to frame, and its shaders count per frame: each frame adds a force from the pointer's move
that frame times min(1/30, g_Frametime)/0.02, and takes at least 1/255 off. Per second the force
is proportional to the frame time, so the water's strength depends on the frame rate — in WE too
(its fps setting: WE draws once per frame, every pass once). LWE ran it once per frame *drawn*:
a scene the host throttles, or a frame that comes late, pushed several frames' force at once —
3184155462's ripple was much stronger than the owner's WE at its 240 fps.

wp_project_set_frame_rate (hz) says the rate the host means to draw at (WE's fps setting, the
rate before any throttling). An effect whose passes read one of its own FBOs before this frame
writes it (last frame's state) and never the layer's picture (`previous`, or texture 0 unbound)
is a simulation: the passes that write its FBOs step at that rate — a late frame runs several
steps, an early one none (at most 32 in a frame) — each with g_Frametime 1/rate, the pointer and
the last pointer along the line from the last step's to this frame's (WE samples the pointer
once per frame and copies it to g_PointerPositionLast after drawing, GH 0x1401b1c0b), and a press
seen between steps on the next step's g_PointerState.z. A frame drawn again at the same time
doesn't step. The passes that draw its picture run every frame. No rate set: one step per frame,
as before. motionblur's accumulation reads `previous`, so it isn't one.

Origin: ours
Why: docs/player/spikes/we-lwe-gap-audit.md "Cursor ripple result" (3184155462)
Status: candidate-for-upstream
Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01Vg3T4kWN88ooeafgoHLbfM
